    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.1",
    "topic_title": "Enzymes",
    "stem": "An enzyme speeds the breakdown of molecule X. Its active site is lined with amino acids whose R groups carry positive charges, and the part of X that binds there carries negative charges. A researcher then tests molecule Y, which has the same size and shape as X but carries positive charges in the part that would contact the active site. Which prediction, with its reasoning, is best supported?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The enzyme will speed the breakdown of X but not of Y, because Y's charges do not complement the active site, so Y cannot form an enzyme-substrate complex in which activation energy is lowered.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Substrate binding requires a fit in both shape and charge. The negative region of X is attracted to the positive active site, so an enzyme-substrate complex forms and activation energy drops. The positive region of Y is repelled by the same R groups, so no complex forms and Y's reaction is not sped up."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The enzyme will speed the breakdown of X and Y equally, because both molecules have the same shape and so fit the active site equally well.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is a shape-only view of specificity. It ignores that the substrate's charge must also match the chemical environment of the active site. Like charges repel, so Y cannot bind the way X does. Fix: For each substrate, check both shape and charge against the active-site R groups before predicting binding."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The enzyme will speed the breakdown of X by supplying energy to X molecules so they can get over the activation energy barrier.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the misconception that enzymes add energy to reactions. Enzymes do not supply energy. They bind the substrate in a way that lowers the activation energy barrier, so more molecules can react at the same energy. Fix: Ask whether your explanation changes the height of the energy barrier or the energy given to molecules, and choose the barrier."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The enzyme will speed the breakdown of X by making the products of X lower in energy, so the reaction releases more energy overall.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses activation energy with the overall energy difference between reactants and products. An enzyme lowers only the energy barrier. The starting and ending energy levels, and so the total energy released, stay the same with or without the enzyme. Fix: On an energy diagram, locate which part an enzyme changes: the peak only, not the reactant or product levels."
     }
    ]
   },
