   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.1",
    "topic_title": "Enzymes",
    "stem": "Two molecules, S and T, are similar in size but differ in shape and charge. In separate tests under identical conditions, adding enzyme E increases the reaction rate for S but does not change the reaction rate for T. Which explanation best connects enzyme structure to these results?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The active site of E is compatible with S in shape and charge, allowing E to supply the activation energy needed for S to react.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Energy-donor misconception: Enzymes do not speed reactions by supplying activation energy; they lower the activation-energy barrier. Fix: Check whether your explanation lowers the energy barrier or incorrectly makes the enzyme an energy source."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The active site of E is compatible with S in shape and charge, allowing an enzyme–substrate complex to form that lowers the reaction’s activation energy.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Active-site shape and charge determine which substrates can bind appropriately. Binding S allows E to lower the activation energy for its reaction, increasing the reaction rate; T’s different shape and charge explain why E does not catalyze its reaction."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The similar sizes of S and T mean both must fit the active site of E, so E should increase both reaction rates.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Size-only specificity trap: Similar size does not guarantee appropriate binding because substrate shape and charge must also be compatible with the active site. Fix: Compare substrate shape and charge with the active site rather than using size alone to predict binding."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The active site of E is compatible with S in shape and charge, allowing an enzyme–substrate complex to form that raises the reaction’s activation energy.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Activation-energy direction reversal: Raising the activation-energy barrier would make the reaction slower under the same conditions, not explain the observed increase in rate. Fix: Trace the relationship from barrier height to reaction rate before deciding whether activation energy rises or falls."
     }
    ]
   },
